// MATHGPL BENCHMARK SOLUTION STANDARD
// Distilled from the 10 benchmark worked solutions in
//   docs/MathGPL Knowledge Base/Benchmark Library/
// Defines the required step granularity. Every solution generated by
// notebook-ai must follow these patterns — no hidden operations, no jumps.

export const BENCHMARK_STANDARD = `
BENCHMARK SOLUTION STANDARD — match the step density of Benchmarks 01–10.
If a student could ask "how did this line become the next?", a step is
MISSING and must be inserted.

GLOBAL RULES (Phase 5):
  • Every coefficient change, denominator change, sign change, bracket
    expansion, bracket factorisation, term move, fraction simplification,
    or variable isolation MUST appear as its own visible line.
  • The very first line of every solution restates the original problem
    verbatim, with no working.
  • The final line is the final answer, stated clearly.

BENCHMARK 01 — SIMULTANEOUS EQUATIONS (elimination/substitution)
  Show label of each equation (① ②), the multiplied form before
  subtracting, the subtraction line, isolation of the variable, back-
  substitution, and the final pair {x = …, y = …}.

BENCHMARK 02 — QUADRATIC EQUATIONS
  Factorising: show the split middle term, the grouping, the common
  factor extracted, then each root on its own line.
  Formula method: write the formula, substitute a, b, c with brackets
  around negatives, simplify the discriminant, then split into the two
  ± roots on separate lines.

BENCHMARK 03 — RATIONAL EQUATIONS
  State the LCM, multiply EACH term by the LCM on its own line, cancel
  denominators line-by-line, then solve as a linear/quadratic. State the
  domain restriction (denominators ≠ 0) and check the answer against it.

BENCHMARK 04 — INEQUALITIES
  Treat like an equation but show the inequality sign on every line.
  When dividing/multiplying by a negative, write a separate line stating
  the sign flip BEFORE flipping. End with interval notation or number-
  line description.

BENCHMARK 05 — LOGARITHMS
  Convert log_{a} b = c into b = a^{c} as a labelled "Recall:" line.
  Combine logs using product/quotient/power rules each on its own line.
  After exponentiating, verify the answer lies in the domain (argument > 0)
  and reject extraneous roots explicitly.

BENCHMARK 06 — TRIGONOMETRY
  State the identity used as a "Recall:" line before applying it.
  Show every algebraic rearrangement of the trig equation as its own line.
  When taking inverse trig, list ALL solutions in the required interval
  on separate lines.

BENCHMARK 07 — DIFFERENTIATION
  Power rule mechanics MUST be shown:
    d/dx(2x²) = 2 × 2x^{2−1} = 2 × 2x^{1} = 4x
  Show the exponent subtraction explicitly. For product/quotient/chain
  rule, label the rule, identify u and v on their own lines, compute
  du/dx and dv/dx separately, then assemble.

BENCHMARK 08 — INTEGRATION
  Show the power increased by one and the division by the new power:
    ∫ 2x² dx = 2 × x^{2+1}/(2+1) + C = 2 × x³/3 + C = (2/3)x³ + C
  For definite integrals, write F(b) and F(a) on separate lines before
  subtracting.

BENCHMARK 09 — PROBABILITY
  Write the sample space size on its own line. Write each event's
  favourable-outcome count on its own line. Then P(event) = favourable /
  total as a stacked fraction, then simplified. For combined events show
  the rule (addition / multiplication / conditional) as a "Recall:" line
  before substituting.

BENCHMARK 10 — STATISTICS
  For mean: write Σx on its own line, write n on its own line, then
  mean = Σx / n as a stacked fraction, then evaluated.
  For variance / standard deviation: show the (x − x̄) column, the
  (x − x̄)² column, the sum, division by n (or n−1), and finally √ of
  the variance — each as its own line.

CRITICAL NEVER-SKIP TRANSITIONS:
  •  ax = b      MUST be followed by   (ax)/a = b/a   THEN   x = b/a
  •  (x+a)(x+b) MUST be expanded via distribution lines, never jumped to
     the trinomial.
  •  A fraction simplification MUST show the cancelled common factor.
`.trim();
